title: b3.1 Mindful Walk
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Eight concepts. Why walk: experiences on foot stick (HFC-01). Why guide attention: the present asks little of memory (HFC-02), and nature draws attention without effort (HFC-03). Why recall the past: remote memories outlast recent ones (HFC-04). Why personalise listening: the familiar calms (HFC-05). What to avoid: elderspeak (HFC-06), surveillance (HFC-07), a robot read as supervisor (HFC-08).

= Human Factors Concept - Walking, place and memory [HFC-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-01
|Name|Walking, place and memory
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
**Hypothesis.** Group observation: moments of a holiday spent walking are remembered much better than moments spent sitting down. No direct study found yet (Open item O-2).

Candidate mechanisms from the literature:

* Active navigation strengthens spatial memory over passive viewing (Chrastil & Warren, 2012)
* A year of walking enlarged the hippocampus and improved memory in older adults (Erickson et al., 2011)
* Actions performed are remembered better than actions heard or seen: the enactment effect (Engelkamp, 1998)
* Walking interviews draw out more place-bound talk than seated ones (Evans & Jones, 2011)
)))
|Concept statement|Experiences on foot are encoded with place, movement and effort, and are recalled better than experiences sitting down. The walk is therapeutic in itself, wherever it leads.
|Human factor addressed|Episodic and spatial memory; orientation; engagement
|Proposed mechanism|Walking binds an experience to a place and to the body's own action. Each extra cue (location, effort, sequence) is another route back to the memory. Physical activity also supports the hippocampus, on which both spatial and episodic memory depend. Side by side, on the move, talk comes easier.
|Source|Chrastil & Warren (2012); Erickson et al. (2011); Engelkamp (1998); Evans & Jones (2011). See References.
|Conditions and population of validity|Mechanisms shown in healthy adults and older adults, not in dementia. Episodic encoding is impaired in Alzheimer's disease; any advantage may shrink with severity. Treat as a hypothesis; UC01-F2-CL3 tests it.
|Predicted Effect type|Better same-day recall of walk events than of seated events; more talk about the walk afterwards
|Associated Measures|M-03 Same-day recall; M-02 Walking minutes and completion

= Human Factors Concept - Present-moment attention [HFC-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-02
|Name|Present-moment attention (mindfulness in dementia)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Literature only.

* Churcher Clarke et al. (2017): adapted mindfulness feasible in care homes
* Berk et al. (2018): review of rationale and early evidence for mindfulness in dementia
* Paller et al. (2015): uncontrolled study; quality of life and mood improved
* Teut et al. (2013): mindful walking reduced stress in distressed adults (not dementia)
)))
|Concept statement|Attention anchored in the present - senses, body, breath - asks little of memory. Short, concrete cues keep a person with dementia with the activity at hand.
|Human factor addressed|Attention; orientation; anxiety; agitation
|Proposed mechanism|Losing the thread (PS-01, PS-04) is a memory failure: where am I, why am I here. A present-moment cue replaces the need to remember with something to notice. Each cue re-anchors; silence between cues gives time. Abstract imagery ("imagine a beach") needs working memory; concrete sensation ("feel the bench") does not.
|Source|Churcher Clarke et al. (2017); Berk et al. (2018); Paller et al. (2015); Teut et al. (2013). See References.
|Conditions and population of validity|Mild to moderate dementia; small samples; programmes adapted (shorter, concrete, repeated). Severe dementia: unknown.
|Predicted Effect type|Longer engagement; fewer early turn-backs; less anxiety; calmer afterwards
|Associated Measures|M-05 Engagement; M-04 Observed affect; M-06 Agitation

= Human Factors Concept - Attention restoration in nature [HFC-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-03
|Name|Attention restoration in nature
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Literature only.

* Kaplan (1995): attention restoration theory - nature's "soft fascination" lets tired attention recover
* Gonzalez & Kirkevold (2014): sensory gardens in dementia care; possible reduction in agitation; weak study designs
)))
|Concept statement|Natural settings draw attention without effort, so tired attention recovers.
|Human factor addressed|Attention fatigue; stress; agitation
|Proposed mechanism|Directed attention tires. Birdsong, moving leaves and water draw attention involuntarily, at no cost. The garden supplies the birdsong, leaves and water the cues mention.
|Source|Kaplan (1995); Gonzalez & Kirkevold (2014). See References.
|Conditions and population of validity|Theory from general populations; dementia evidence of low quality.
|Predicted Effect type|Calmer during and after the walk; more noticing
|Associated Measures|M-04 Observed affect; M-05 Engagement

= Human Factors Concept - Reminiscence and personhood [HFC-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-04
|Name|Reminiscence and personhood
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Literature only.

* Woods et al. (2018), Cochrane review: small, setting-dependent benefits for quality of life, communication and mood
* Rubin et al. (1998): the reminiscence bump - events from roughly age 10 to 30 are recalled best
* Kitwood (1997): personhood in dementia care
)))
|Concept statement|Remote memories outlast recent ones. Inviting them affirms identity and gives conversation a subject.
|Human factor addressed|Autobiographical memory; identity; communication; mood
|Proposed mechanism|Recent memory fails first; youth and early adulthood remain. A cue from that period - a place, a trade, a song - opens a memory the person can still tell. Telling it to a listener affirms who they are. Testing it ("Do you remember?") exposes the gaps instead.
|Source|Woods et al. (2018); Rubin et al. (1998); Kitwood (1997). See References.
|Conditions and population of validity|Benefits small and variable. Mostly group sessions studied, not walks. Can stir grief or longing (VT-03).
|Predicted Effect type|More engagement and talk; positive affect; at times sadness
|Associated Measures|M-05 Engagement; M-04 Observed affect

= Human Factors Concept - Personalised listening [HFC-05] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-05
|Name|Personalised listening
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Literature only.

* Gerdner (2000): individualised music reduced agitation more than classical "relaxation" music
* Thomas et al. (2017): personalised music programme; fewer behavioural symptoms, less antipsychotic use
* Rubin et al. (1998): favourites cluster in early adulthood
)))
|Concept statement|Audio from the person's own history calms and engages more than generic content.
|Human factor addressed|Agitation; engagement; mood
|Proposed mechanism|Familiar content is recognised with little effort; recognition brings pleasure and a sense of safety. Unfamiliar content demands effort, or is ignored.
|Source|Gerdner (2000); Thomas et al. (2017); Rubin et al. (1998). See References.
|Conditions and population of validity|Shown for music. Transfer to spoken content (news, audiobooks) unproven.
|Predicted Effect type|Offers accepted; longer listening; less agitation
|Associated Measures|M-11 Listening uptake; M-06 Agitation

= Human Factors Concept - Elderspeak and personhood [HFC-06] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-06
|Name|Elderspeak and personhood
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Literature only.

* Williams et al. (2009): elderspeak by nursing-home staff linked to more resistiveness to care
* Kitwood (1997): infantilisation and outpacing erode personhood
)))
|Concept statement|Simplified, sing-song, over-caring speech is heard as talking down, and provokes resistance.
|Human factor addressed|Experienced dignity; resistance; withdrawal
|Proposed mechanism|Speech style signals standing. Endearments, exaggerated intonation and praise for small acts tell an adult they are seen as a child. Meditation scripts share these markers.
|Source|Williams et al. (2009); Kitwood (1997). See References.
|Conditions and population of validity|Care-staff speech in US nursing homes. Robot or recorded voices: not studied.
|Predicted Effect type|Resistance, withdrawal; descriptions of being talked down to
|Associated Measures|M-08 Perceived privacy and tone (code T); M-04 Observed affect

= Human Factors Concept - Perceived surveillance [HFC-07] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-07
|Name|Perceived surveillance
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Literature only.

* Niemeijer et al. (2010): review of surveillance technology in residential dementia care
* Niemeijer et al. (2015): residents' own experience - supports autonomy only within a person-centred approach
)))
|Concept statement|Monitoring felt as continuous, or as more than needed, reads as surveillance and is rejected.
|Human factor addressed|Perceived privacy; acceptance
|Proposed mechanism|Awareness of being tracked makes support feel like supervision.
|Source|Niemeijer et al. (2010, 2015). See References.
|Conditions and population of validity|Residential dementia care; transfers directly.
|Predicted Effect type|Discomfort, avoidance, rejection of the device
|Associated Measures|M-08 Perceived privacy and tone (codes W, R)

= Human Factors Concept - Robot embodiment and role [HFC-08] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HFC-08
|Name|Robot embodiment and role
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|(((
Literature only.

* Pemberton et al. (2026): older adults cast humanoid robots as "Warden"; prefer tool or pet framings
* Furlanis et al. (2026): bias enacted through gaze, timing and tone (conceptual)
* Bodala et al. (2021); Alimardani et al. (2020): Pepper as mindfulness coach, healthy adults
)))
|Concept statement|Form and behaviour decide the role a robot is given. A humanoid leading meditation can be read as a coach, or as a supervisor.
|Human factor addressed|Perceived role; perceived surveillance; dignity; acceptance
|Proposed mechanism|People assign roles to a robot from its body. Gaze that follows and pacing that checks read as watching. A voice that leads, then falls silent, reads as a guide.
|Source|Pemberton et al. (2026); Furlanis et al. (2026); Bodala et al. (2021); Alimardani et al. (2020). See References.
|Conditions and population of validity|Cognitively intact adults; no dementia data.
|Predicted Effect type|Acceptance or rejection; feeling watched
|Associated Measures|M-08 Perceived privacy and tone; M-05 Engagement

